course: dictionary
lesson: 2663
title: One word, two meanings: Devon
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A capital letter is a clue. «Devon» with a big D is a name, and here it is a place: a county in south-west England. The Sea Rat's voyage ends at «the harbours of Cornwall and Devon», so it must be a place.
explain: Look at the words around it. «A wild beaver family on the River Otter in Devon» is about rivers and animals, so Devon is a place where they live. «Devon and tomato sauce sandwiches» is about lunch, so devon there is a food, and it has a small d.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. A county fits «the harbours of Cornwall and Devon», but a sausage does not. If your meaning makes no sense, try another one.
fact: A capital letter at the start of a word often means it is a name, such as a place or a person.
fact: Devon is a county in south-west England, known for moors, beaches and farms.
fact: In Australia, devon is a common noun: a large, mild sausage cut into thin slices for sandwiches.
fact: Devon is also a person's name.
fact: Context tells you which meaning fits, so always read the words around the word.
exercise: «A wild beaver family on the River Otter in Devon was allowed to stay in 2020.» What is Devon here? | *A county in south-west England | A river that flows to the sea | A large, mild sausage | A type of beaver
exercise: Jess's lunchbox has «devon and tomato sauce sandwiches». What is devon in this sentence? | *A large, mild sausage cut into thin slices | A county with moors and beaches | A person's name | A kind of tomato
exercise: «Over lunch the Sea Rat tells the rest: the ports of Portugal and France, the harbours of Cornwall and Devon.» Which clue shows that Devon is a name? | The colon after the word rest | The word and between the places | *The capital D at the start | The word lunch before it
test: Devon waved from the bus stop as we drove past. What kind of word is Devon here? | A county in England | *A person's name | A sausage | A harbour
test: In 2013 Mid Devon council tried a similar ban. What kind of word is Devon here? | A person's name, like a friend | A harbour full of boats | A sausage, in lower case | *A place name, with a capital letter
exam: Read «devon and tomato sauce sandwiches» and «the harbours of Cornwall and Devon». Why is devon written with a small d in one and a big D in the other? | *The first is a food, a common noun; the second is a place name | The first is a name, the second is a food | Capitals are only used at the start of a sentence | Both are places, so both need capitals
